<GuideLayout slug="signs-a-tree-needs-to-be-removed">
  <p class="note">
    <strong>Short answer:</strong> a tree usually needs to come down when it is dead, has a split or cracked trunk,
    leans suddenly or has lifted roots, is badly damaged by a storm, or is decaying at the base. A tree that only looks
    bare, lopsided or messy can often be saved by pruning, so get an arborist's opinion before you decide.
  </p>

  <h2>1. Dead or dying canopy</h2>
  <p>
    Brown, bare or brittle branches out of season, large dead limbs, or bark that is peeling off in sheets are signs the
    tree is failing. Scratch a small twig: green underneath means it is alive, dry and brown means it is dead. A few dead
    twigs are normal; a canopy that is mostly dead is not.
  </p>

  <h2>2. A lean that is new or getting worse</h2>
  <p>
    Many trees grow at an angle and are stable. A tree that has started to lean, especially after a storm, with soil
    lifting on the side opposite the lean or cracks in the ground around the base, may be uprooting and can fall without
    warning.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Cracks, splits and weak joints</h2>
  <p>
    Deep vertical cracks in the trunk, a split where two trunks join, or a limb hanging by a thread of bark are
    structural defects. Forks with bark squeezed between the two stems are weak and tend to tear apart in wind.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Decay: mushrooms, hollows and soft wood</h2>
  <p>
    Mushrooms or shelf-like fungus on the trunk or at the base, cavities, oozing sap, or wood that is soft and crumbly
    mean the inside of the tree is rotting. A hollow tree can look healthy and still break.
  </p>

  <h2>5. Root problems</h2>
  <p>
    Roots that are cut, exposed or lifted, soil mounding up on one side, or damage from digging, paving or construction
    near the tree can leave it without enough support. Roots that damage the foundation, pool, pipes or pavement are a
    problem for the property even when the tree is healthy.
  </p>

  <h2>6. Storm damage</h2>
  <p>
    A tree that has lost a large part of its canopy, has broken main limbs or has a split trunk after a hurricane or
    storm is often unsafe. Photograph the damage before anything is moved, for your insurance claim, and keep away from
    any tree touching a power line. See <a href="/tree-care-guides/hurricane-tree-preparation/">how to prepare your
    trees for hurricane season</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>7. Disease and pests</h2>
  <p>
    Thinning leaves, yellowing, dieback, boring holes or sawdust at the base point to disease or insects. In palms, a
    newest frond that collapses or turns brown, or fronds that yellow and die from the bottom up, can signal a serious
    problem. An arborist can identify the cause and tell you whether treatment is possible.
  </p>

  <h2>8. A tree that is too close to what it can damage</h2>
  <p>
    A healthy tree can still be the wrong tree in the wrong place: limbs over the roof, roots against a foundation, or
    branches reaching power lines. Sometimes trimming solves it, and sometimes removal is the only fix.
  </p>

  <h2>What to do next</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Do not climb or cut</strong> a damaged tree yourself, and keep people and cars away from it.</li>
    <li><strong>Get an arborist's assessment</strong> if you are not sure. It can show that the tree is safe, or document the risk. See <a href="/certified-arborist/">arborist assessments</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Check the permit rules.</strong> Under Florida Statute 163.045, a homeowner of a detached single-family home may remove a tree without a local permit when an ISA certified arborist or Florida licensed landscape architect documents an unacceptable risk. See <a href="/tree-removal-permits/">our permit guide</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Get a free estimate</strong> for <a href="/tree-removal/">tree removal</a>, or send an <a href="/emergency-tree-removal/">emergency request</a> if the tree is on a structure or near power lines.</li>
  </ul>
</GuideLayout>
